From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Prefer software encoding over VA-API

Stopping a cast can abort inside the VA-API media driver: a free() in the
driver, reached from vaTerminate() as GStreamer releases the VA
context, reported by glibc as "double free or corruption".

This was first seen with an earlier build and blamed on heap corruption
elsewhere in this project. That corruption was real -- a g_autofree variable
freed without ever being initialised, in the PulseAudio teardown -- and
fixing it was worth doing. Hardware encoding was then restored on the
argument that the driver had been the victim rather than the cause. The
abort came back, with a stack that reaches libc's allocator directly from
the driver's own teardown and passes through nothing belonging to this
project.

Nothing in this process can prevent a fault in that code, so the encoder
that does not reach it is selected first. VA encoders remain in the table as
fallbacks for machines without x264. The cost is real, since software
encoding takes far more processor time than a hardware encoder, but a cast
that ends without taking the session down is worth more.

--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
@@ -50,6 +50,21 @@
 /* video encoder, audio encoder, muxer */
 static const WfdMediaFactoryProfile wfd_media_factory_profiles[] = {
   /* video + audio profiles */
+  /* Software encoding first.
+   *
+   * Stopping a cast aborted inside the VA-API media driver: a free() in the
+   * driver, reached from vaTerminate() when GStreamer released the VA
+   * context, with the message "double free or corruption". This was blamed
+   * once on heap corruption elsewhere in this project, which did exist and
+   * was fixed; hardware encoding was then restored and the abort came back
+   * with the fault entirely inside the driver's own teardown. Nothing in
+   * this process can prevent it, so the encoder that does not reach that
+   * code is used. VA encoders stay as fallbacks for machines without x264.
+   */
+  {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_X264,       ELEMENT_AAC_FDK,    ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
+  {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_X264,       ELEMENT_AAC_AVENC,  ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
+  {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_X264,       ELEMENT_AAC_FAAC,   ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
+  {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_X264,       ELEMENT_AUDIO_NONE, ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
   {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_VAH264,     ELEMENT_AAC_FDK,    ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
   {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_VAH264,     ELEMENT_AAC_AVENC,  ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
   {PROFILE_HIGH_H264,    ELEMENT_VAH264,     ELEMENT_AAC_FAAC,   ELEMENT_MPEGTS},
